   "source": [
    "# Lab 02 (solution): RL from scratch\n",
    "\n",
    "Reference implementation. Two learning signals built by hand: value-based RL (Q-learning on a gridworld) and preference-based reward modeling (the Bradley-Terry core of RLHF). Concepts: [rl-primitives](../../../concepts/rl/rl-primitives.md), [rlhf](../../../concepts/rl/rlhf.md)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 0: Q-learning on a gridworld"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "sys.path.insert(0, \"..\")  # modules (qlearning.py, preferences.py) live in the lab root\n",
    "from qlearning import train, greedy_path, render_policy, OPTIMAL_STEPS\n",
    "# RL has three parts: an environment (the grid), a reward (+10 at goal, -1 per step), and a policy\n",
    "# learned from values. Q-learning fills a table Q[state][action] and acts greedily on it.\n",
    "Q = train(seed=0)\n",
    "print(render_policy(Q))\n",
    "path = greedy_path(Q)\n",
    "print(f\"\\npath: {path}  ({len(path)-1} steps, optimal={OPTIMAL_STEPS})\")\n"
    "print(f\"\\npath: {path}  ({len(path)-1} steps, optimal={OPTIMAL_STEPS})\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 1: value is long-run, not immediate"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# The \"value\" is long-run, not immediate: the start state has positive value because the policy from\n",
    "# there eventually reaches the +10 goal, despite paying step penalties on the way.\n",
    "print(\"value of best action at start:\", round(max(Q[0]), 2))\n"
    "print(\"value of best action at start:\", round(max(Q[0]), 2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2: reward modeling from preferences (RLHF core)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from preferences import make_preferences, fit_reward, ranking, TRUE_QUALITY\n",
    "# RLHF's engine: you can't write a reward for \"better answer,\" so you learn one from comparisons.\n",
    "# Bradley-Terry: P(i preferred over j) = sigmoid(r_i - r_j). Fit r to the preferences.\n",
    "prefs = make_preferences(TRUE_QUALITY)        # only \"A beats B\" pairs - no scores\n",
    "r = fit_reward(prefs, len(TRUE_QUALITY))\n",
    "print(f\"{len(prefs)} pairwise prefs -> learned reward:\", [round(x,2) for x in r])\n",
    "print(\"learned ranking:\", ranking(r), \" true ranking:\", ranking(TRUE_QUALITY))\n"
    "print(\"learned ranking:\", ranking(r), \" true ranking:\", ranking(TRUE_QUALITY))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3: the policy picks the best option"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from preferences import policy_choice\n",
    "# The policy optimized against the reward model picks the top-scored option - learned with no labels,\n",
    "# only preferences. This is the loop RLHF runs at scale: preferences -> reward model -> policy.\n",
    "print(\"policy picks item:\", policy_choice(r), \"(true best:\", ranking(TRUE_QUALITY)[0], \")\")\n"
    "print(\"policy picks item:\", policy_choice(r), \"(true best:\", ranking(TRUE_QUALITY)[0], \")\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## What you built\n",
    "\n",
    "The two learning signals behind modern model training, from scratch. **Q-learning** is value-based RL: an agent learns Q[state][action] by trial and reward and acts greedily, converging to the optimal six-step route - environment, reward, and policy made concrete. **Reward modeling** is the engine inside RLHF: from pairwise preferences alone (no scores), a Bradley-Terry fit recovers a reward that orders the options, and a greedy policy picks the best. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Where this simplifies:** the gridworld is tiny and deterministic, so a table suffices; real RL uses function approximation (a network) over enormous state spaces and methods like PPO for stability. The reward model here fits noiseless preferences over five fixed items; in RLHF the items are model outputs, the preferences are noisy human labels, and a policy is then optimized against the learned reward with a KL penalty to stay close to the base model. Concepts: [rl-primitives](../../../concepts/rl/rl-primitives.md), [policy-gradients](../../../concepts/rl/policy-gradients.md), [rlhf](../../../concepts/rl/rlhf.md)."
   ]
  }
